When an element with a hidden backface faces away, Ladybird culls
everything flattened into its plane, including any 3D rendering context
established inside it. A participant in a 3D rendering context is culled
only by the backface of its own plane, or by whatever culls the plane
its context is flattened into.

Gecko does the same: WebRender’s PictureInstance::is_visible()
rejects a back-facing picture outside preserve-3d along with its
contents. Safari renders the same result: WebKit’s GraphicsLayerCA::
updateBackfaceVisibility() makes the hidden element’s layer
single-sided, and a nested context painted into that layer goes with it,
unless the context gets a compositing layer of its own.

Blink differs on purpose: FragmentPaintPropertyTreeBuilder::
UpdateIndividualTransform() makes the backface visible again for any
element that establishes a new rendering context, so Chrome keeps
nested contexts painted under a flipped hidden backface — which its
Preserve3DUnderBackfaceHiddenWithout3DTransforms test pins down. The
CSSWG hasn’t settled which behavior is right:
https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/918
